# Verdict: READY WITH CHANGES

No P1 code defect was found. The lowering, allocation, liveness dataflow, validator, and lift are correct and internally consistent; I hand-re-derived every live-set fixture and confirmed each asserted value is right. Two should-fix gaps (P2) and two minor notes (P3) follow.

## P1 — none.

## P2 — the design document has no section 4.5, yet the code and tests cite it as their spec

`docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md` jumps straight from §4.4 to §5. There is no §4.5 anywhere in the file (confirmed by grep: the word "live" appears only at lines 71, 311, 321, in unrelated senses). The entire live-register-set feature this commit is titled for — contract version 1→2, `:call`'s sixth `live` operand, `body-liveness`, the four live-set validator rules, and the §4.5 "work list" the five fixtures implement — is implemented and tested but absent from the authoritative spec.

Concretely, the spec and the shipped format now diverge:
- §4.4 (line 339) lists `:call [op rd fn-reg arg-regs tail?]` — five operands. The code's `opcode-table` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:125-129`) ships six, adding `[:yin.debruijn.register/live :data]`.
- §3's descriptor "Validation" row (line 238) reads `Shape, targets, body scope, register bounds` — no live-set rules, no version-2 note.
- The code's `contract-version` docstring (lines 63-74) and `body-liveness` docstring (line 245) both cite "design section 4.5" as if it existed.

This is a genuine spec/implementation divergence on a format that the project treats as design-first ("non-negotiable invariants"). It is the one place a single-session implementer could have silently moved the spec without the owner noticing — and here the spec was *not* updated at all. The commit touches only `src/` and `test/` (4 files), no `docs/` edit.

## P2 — the validator does not reject a cross-body `:jump`/`:branch-false`

`body-scope-rule` (`debruijn_register_code.cljc:427-466`) checks body shape, contiguity, coverage, and closure targets — but never checks that a `:jump` or `:branch-false` target lies inside its own body's `[start end]`. `target-bounds-rule` (413-424) only bounds targets against the whole vector length. So a hand-built image with a `:jump` from body 0 into body 1's interior passes every rule, and `body-liveness` then silently computes wrong liveness (it reads the cross-body successor's `live-in`, which is empty because each body's pass updates only its own pcs). The wrong value is consistent, so `:live-exact` does not catch it either.

This diverges from B1's direct precedent: the stack-image validator rejects the same malformation via `scope-defect`/`all-body-chains`'s `:scope-conflict` (a pc reached under two different scope chains). The register validator has the body ranges (`owner-of`) available and should check each control-transfer target is within its own body. Latent today — the lowerer never emits such an image and there is no receive/kernel path yet — but the validator is explicitly positioned as the sole admitter for a future kernel, which would otherwise jump into another body's register file.

